ors like senator Stennis  of Mississippi and others state why they  are opposed to statehood for why  principally because of dilution of the  Congress is that where most of the  Opposition in Congress comes from from  southern senators yes the principal and  the most serious opposition comes from  the senators from the South who want to  maintain the traditional position of the  south with respect to certain types of  legislation under the present rules of  the Senate which permit unlimited debate  they have what amounts to the power to  veto legislation to which they  very serious objections now what if  ardently mr. fountain I had one senator  tell me that he was opposed to statehood  for Hawaii because we might have an  oriental senator what do you think of  that is that a possibility and what do  you think of it if it is a possibility I  want to answer that by saying that no  one will represent Hawaiian Congress who  first of all is not an American citizen  and I don't think it is a proper to  raise any questions regarding their  ancestral background any more than it is  to do the same with the people of the  States what about loyalty to the United  States did you during the second war for  instance did you have any was disloyalty  a great problem in Hawaii no it was not  and on the contrary the record of Hawaii  of fully vindicated the confidence that  we had in the Americans of Oriental  ancestry I want to say in part with  further reference in your question mr.  Cromwell that that of course is within  the realm of possibility and the weight  of Hawaii would not offer any objection  to a man because of his race I think  that if someone came along who met the  requirements of the of such a position  that he would be selected when I lived  in Hawai before they wore encountered  what I thought was a very remarkable  situation there and that was in the  shape of these Japanese language schools  they still have those Japanese language  schools know the Japanese lan